<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope John Paul II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Karol Józef Wojtyła, John Paul II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope John Paul II, born Karol Józef Wojtyła in Wadowice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, was head of the Catholic Church and sovereign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a> from 16 October 1978 until his death in 2005, the first non-Italian pope since Adrian VI in the 16th century. Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> from 1964, he visited 129 countries as pope and canonised 483 saints. His 1979 visit to Poland contributed to the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> movement in 1980, and he has been credited with helping to end communist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He survived being shot by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Mehmet Ali</a> Ağca in 1981, and has been criticised over his response to the sexual abuse of children by priests. He was canonised in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii.jpg" alt="Pope John Paul II" width="330" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gregorini Demetrio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ADAMELLO_-_PAPA_-_Giovanni_Paolo_II_-_panoramio_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church’s ban on Copernican books was relaxed only gradually: the general prohibition was dropped in 1758, and Copernicus’s book and Galileo’s Dialogue left the Index of Prohibited Books in 1835; in 1992 <b>Pope John Paul II</b> acknowledged the errors of Galileo’s judges.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>That same year, 1979, <b>Pope John Paul II</b>, a native of Poland, visited the country; a figure Poles could identify with, beyond the regime’s reach, he was later named by Lech Wałęsa as a significant factor in Solidarity’s creation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…and 1970s it was sung at Catholic folk masses and as a hymn in Protestant churches; in 1997 <b>Pope John Paul II</b> drew on it before some 300,000 young Italian Catholics at a church congress where Dylan performed, and in 2007 Pope Benedict XVI wrote that he had…</blockquote><a href="/songs/blowin-in-the-wind">Blowin' in the Wind</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Paul II</b>, the first non-Italian pope in over four centuries, died in the Vatican.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-2">2 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The move reflected close cooperation with <b>Pope John Paul II</b> during the Cold War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Paul II</b> recovered and later visited his attacker in prison to forgive him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Holy Father John Paul II Family Home in Wadowice, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.88,19.49&amp;year=1975" aria-label="Holy Father John Paul II Family Home in Wadowice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4997 428 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope John Paul II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5447 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5447 698h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.88,19.49&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
